[Event "Scotch Gambit: the gambit itself"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/89PLb4a2/9yI8g1Um"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis"]

{ Today we learn the Scotch Gambit. You give a pawn away on purpose, tear the centre wide open, and point everything at f seven while he is still tidying up. [%cal Ye2e4] }
1. e4 { Your king pawn steps forward two squares, opening a road for the queen and a road for the light bishop in a single move. } (1. d4 { The queen pawn opening is every bit as strong a first move, but it leads to a completely different kind of game. } 1... Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 d5 { This is the queen pawn game, calm and closed. Today we are after the sharp, open fight instead. }) 1... e5 { He answers you head on, planting his own pawn on e five. Bring the king knight out next and put that pawn under attack right away. [%cal Yg1f3] } 2. Nf3 { Your knight leaps out and attacks his pawn on e five directly, so he now has to spend his next move answering the threat. [%cal Gf3e5] } (2. Bc4 { The bishop loves the square c four too, and this is a perfectly fine move — but developing the knight with a threat comes first. } 2... Nf6 3. Nc3 Nc6 4. d3 Bb4 { This gives a sound, quiet game with easy development. Today, though, we are after the sharper central pawn break. }) (2. d4 { The centre game. Playable, but this move order lets him swap off a bishop with check before you get moving. } 2... exd4 3. Nf3 Bb4+ 4. Bd2 Bxd2+ { The bishops trade off here and the position is perfectly fine, just a lot calmer and less fun than the line we are after today. }) (2. Nc3 { Developing the other knight first is also a perfectly good try, but it does not attack anything of his just yet. } 2... Nf6 3. Nf3 Nc6 4. Bb5 Nd4 { This reaches the four knights game, solid and well studied — a fine opening, but a different lesson for another day. }) 2... Nc6 { He defends the pawn with his own knight, guarding it comfortably. Now is the moment to break the centre open with your queen pawn. [%cal Yd2d4] } 3. d4 { Your pawn marches right into the centre, offering itself up on purpose so the whole position blasts open for your pieces. [%csl Gd4] } (3. Bb5 { The Spanish bishop check is one of the strongest tries in all of chess, but the theory behind it runs enormously deep. } 3... a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O b5 { This is genuinely excellent chess and a fine weapon to learn one day — just not the lesson we are here for today. }) (3. Bc4 { The Italian bishop move here is perfectly good chess, but it leads to a much slower, quieter kind of game. } 3... Bc5 4. d3 Nf6 5. c3 d6 { This settles into a long, quiet manoeuvring game. Today we are chasing something sharper and more open. }) (3. Nc3 { Developing the other knight is a fine try too, but it lets him plant his own knight comfortably on d four. } 3... Nf6 4. Bb5 Nd4 5. Nxd4 exd4 { This is perfectly playable chess, but the central pawn break you were building toward has already slipped away. }) 3... exd4 { He grabs the free pawn without much thought. Now comes the gambit itself: swing your bishop to c four, staring straight at f seven. [%cal Yf1c4] } 4. Bc4 { There is the gambit. He keeps the pawn on d four; you have a bishop glaring at f seven, a knight in the middle and an open road for your rook. [%csl Gf7] } (4. Nxd4 { Taking the pawn straight back with the knight is the plain Scotch Game, and it is a shade safer according to the books. } 4... Nf6 5. Nxc6 bxc6 6. e5 Qe7 { This is sound, solid chess with a small, lasting edge, but there is no gambit here and none of the sharp fun we are chasing today. }) (4. Qxd4 { Look again before playing this: his own knight on c six is still watching d four closely, ready to jump in and grab your queen there. } 4... Nxd4 5. Nxd4 d5 { Your queen is gone, and a knight and a pawn is not payment for her. Count the guards of a square before you put your queen on it. }) (4. c3 { The Danish idea. Playable, but you hand over a second pawn to get it. } 4... dxc3 5. Nxc3 Bc5 6. Bc4 d6 { Fun, and only a single pawn spent in the end — sound enough, just not our sharper line today. }) 1-0


[Event "Scotch Gambit: the main line with e5"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/89PLb4a2/Ss79RpcV"]
[FEN "r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/8/2BpP3/5N2/PPP2PPP/RNBQK2R b KQkq - 1 4"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/8/2BpP3/5N2/PPP2PPP/RNBQK2R_b_KQkq_-_1_4"]

{ His most popular answer to the gambit: the king knight jumps out and pokes at your pawn on e four. }
4... Nf6 { Out comes his knight to f six, putting your pawn on e four straight under fire. What is the right way to answer it? } 5. e5 { Push the pawn forward instead of guarding it — it kicks his knight away and grabs a whole chunk of central space in one move. [%csl Ge5] } (5. O-O { Castling straight away is every bit as sound and well known, tucking your king safely away while development continues. } 5... Nxe4 6. Re1 d5 7. Bxd5 Qxd5 8. Nc3 Qd8 { This reaches a sharp, well-tested position that is perfectly playable. Today, though, the pawn push is our line. }) (5. Nxd4 { Grabbing the pawn back with the knight looks safe and simple, but his pieces actually move faster than yours here. } 5... Nxe4 6. Bxf7+ Kxf7 7. Qh5+ g6 8. Qd5+ Kg7 { Sharp and genuinely unclear, and by the numbers he stands a shade better here. }) (5. Ng5 { The knight lunges straight at f seven, hunting for a quick trick, but he simply shuts the door with his own d pawn. } 5... d5 6. exd5 Qe7+ 7. Kf1 Ne5 { Your king is stuck in the middle with no way to castle, and the position is simply worse for you. }) 5... d5 { He leaves the attacked knight where it stands and counter-punches: his pawn on d five hits your bishop on c four. Where does the bishop belong? } 6. Bb5 { Lovely square — the bishop steps aside to b five and pins his knight on c six squarely against his own king. [%cal Gb5c6,Gb5e8] } (6. exf6 { The free knight is the trap: while you take it, his pawn takes your bishop on c four. [%cal Rd5c4] } 6... dxc4 7. fxg7 Bxg7 { His bishop rakes the long diagonal from g seven, he is a pawn up, and your king is still stuck in the middle. [%cal Rg7a1] }) (6. Bd3 { A safe enough retreat, but the bishop sits on d three doing nothing at all — no pin, no pressure, no real job. } 6... Ne4 7. Nbd2 Nxd2 8. Bxd2 Bg4 { Material stays level, and your pieces are left with no real bite anywhere on the board. }) (6. Bb3 { Tucking the bishop away to b three lets his knight jump straight into e four completely unopposed. } 6... Ne4 7. O-O Bg4 8. c3 Bc5 { He ends up better here, and your own bishop is boxed in behind its own pawns with nowhere useful to go. }) (6. exd6 { Capturing in passing is legal here, but it rips the position wide open in a way that mainly helps him. } 6... Bxd6 7. Qe2+ Be7 8. Bg5 O-O { His pieces come flying into the game far faster than yours, and the position favours him. }) 6... Ne4 { His knight hops boldly into e four, deep in your territory. Do not panic about it — ask instead what you can win back. } 7. Nxd4 { Yes — the knight takes the pawn back and lands in the middle, leaning on his pinned knight on c six for a second time. [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd4c6] } (7. Bxc6+ { The trade is coming, but not this move: his bishop on c eight has not committed itself anywhere yet. } 7... bxc6 8. Nxd4 Ba6 { From a six the bishop watches f one, so your king cannot castle, and his two bishops own the open board. [%cal Ra6f1] }) (7. O-O { Castling first is safe and sound, but it leaves the pawn on d four in his hands for at least one more move. } 7... Bg4 8. c3 dxc3 9. Nxc3 Nxc3 { This stays perfectly playable, but along the way you never actually get the pawn back at all. }) (7. c3 { Trying to win the pawn back with a pawn move lets him simply grab it and trade pieces off in reply. } 7... dxc3 8. Nxc3 Bb4 9. O-O Bxc3 { Your central pawn presence has disappeared entirely, and he is left perfectly comfortable. }) (7. Nbd2 { Chasing his knight with a second one looks natural, but it only leads to a simple trade of pieces. } 7... Bc5 8. Qe2 Nxd2 9. Qxd2 O-O { Material stays level, and by now the pin you built earlier on c six has melted away completely. }) 7... Bd7 { His bishop comes to d seven purely to defend the pinned knight. Is there a good trade sitting on the board right now? } 8. Bxc6 { Nice — the bishop takes the knight. However he takes back, two of his pawns end up stacked on the c column for the rest of the game. [%csl Rc6,Rc7] } (8. O-O { Castling here is safe enough, but the moment to damage his pawn structure with the trade has already gone. } 8... Nxd4 9. Bxd7+ Qxd7 10. Qxd4 Bc5 { Material stays level, and his pieces settle onto comfortable squares with nothing to complain about. }) (8. Nxc6 { Recapturing with the wrong piece here leaves your bishop on b five with no job left to do at all. } 8... bxc6 9. Bd3 Qe7 10. Qe2 Qxe5 { He wins his pawn back cleanly and ends up standing better, with your pieces less active than his. }) (8. Nb3 { Retreating the knight here serves no real purpose, and it hands him a free move to bring his queen into play. } 8... Qh4 9. O-O O-O-O 10. Bxc6 Bxc6 { By now all of his pieces are developed and active, while several of yours are still sitting at home. }) 8... bxc6 { His b pawn takes back, and now he has two pawns stacked on the c column. Your own king has been standing in the middle a while now. } 9. O-O { Well played — your king tucks away on g one, your rook lands on f one, and those doubled c pawns are a weakness he can never repair. [%csl Rc6,Rc7] } (9. Nc3 { Bringing out the last knight looks like natural development, but your own king is still sitting exposed in the centre. } 9... Qe7 10. Bf4 Qh4 11. Be3 Bb4 { He gets his own pieces moving first — castling your king to safety really does come before anything else. }) (9. Nf3 { Stepping the knight back to f three gives up the strong central square it had just won, for no real gain. } 9... Bc5 10. O-O O-O 11. Qd3 Be7 { Material stays equal, and you have simply spent two whole moves shuffling the same knight back and forth. }) (9. Bf4 { Developing the bishop looks natural, but it lets his queen arrive on h four with an immediate hit on your position. } 9... Qh4 10. Be3 Qe7 11. f3 Nd6 { This works out fine for him — castling your own king to safety really did need to come first. }) 1-0


[Event "Scotch Gambit: the knight jumps to e4"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/89PLb4a2/KAuAKn3c"]
[FEN "r1bqkb1r/pppp1ppp/2n2n2/4P3/2Bp4/5N2/PPP2PPP/RNBQK2R b KQkq - 0 5"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqkb1r/pppp1ppp/2n2n2/4P3/2Bp4/5N2/PPP2PPP/RNBQK2R_b_KQkq_-_0_5"]

{ Last time he counter-punched with d five. This time the knight leaps into your half of the board instead, looking for trouble. }
5... Ne4 { His knight jumps boldly to e four instead of blocking with a pawn. What is the right way to meet it here? } 6. Qe2 { Good — your queen comes to e two and looks along the e column at the knight, which nothing of his guards. It has to run. [%cal Ge2e4] } (6. O-O { Castling straight away is also excellent here, and by the tiniest of margins it is even the more precise choice. } 6... Be7 7. Re1 d5 8. exd6 Nxd6 9. Bd5 { This reaches a perfectly fine game as well, but the queen move is the one we want to make our own. }) (6. Bd5 { Chasing the knight with the bishop is playable enough, but the bishop itself starts to drift away from the action. } 6... Nc5 7. O-O Be7 8. c3 dxc3 { Material stays equal here, but your own bishop has wandered a long way from where it is needed. }) (6. c3 { Grabbing the pawn back with a pawn move here is too hasty — his central pawn break gets to come first instead. } 6... d5 7. exd6 Nxd6 8. Bd3 Qe7+ { His queen swings out with check, and the resulting position leaves you clearly worse off. }) 6... Nc5 { The knight hops back to c five, eyeing your bishop on c four. Stop and ask yourself what you have not done yet. } 7. O-O { Yes — tuck your king away into safety, bring the rook into the game, and let the stray pawn on d four wait its turn. } (7. c3 { Grabbing the pawn back straight away is also good, and every bit as sound a choice as castling first. } 7... d3 8. Qe3 Be7 9. b4 Ne6 { This stays perfectly playable, but tucking your king away first is simply the easier plan to carry out. }) (7. Ng5 { The knight lunges at f seven yet again, but he simply covers the square, and you have gained nothing for the trip. } 7... Ne6 8. Nxe6 dxe6 9. O-O Bd7 { Material stays equal, but you have traded off the best knight you had for one of his worse ones. }) (7. Bd5 { Chasing the knight with the bishop again means the bishop is doing all of his defensive work for him. } 7... b6 8. c4 dxc3 9. Nxc3 Ba6 { Your genuinely good light bishop ends up traded off for one of his lesser pieces — no bargain at all. }) 7... Ne6 { His knight settles comfortably on e six, and that little pawn on d four is still sitting there, still not yours. } 8. c3 { Good — the little pawn steps forward, ready to recapture on d four next move and rebuild your whole centre. [%cal Gc3d4] } (8. Bxe6 { By the raw numbers this trade is just as good, but it hands him the open f column to use for his own pieces. } 8... fxe6 9. Rd1 Be7 10. Nxd4 Nxd4 { Material stays equal, and his rook now has a wide open road down the f column. }) (8. Rd1 { Lining the rook up on the pawn looks purposeful, but it works far more slowly than simply pushing the pawn. } 8... d5 9. exd6 Bxd6 10. c3 O-O { This position stays perfectly fine, just a small shade worse than the line where the pawn move comes first. }) (8. Re1 { The rook picks entirely the wrong open column here — nothing of any consequence is happening along it. } 8... Be7 9. c3 O-O 10. Qe4 d5 { He settles into a comfortable position while your own pieces are left drifting without a clear plan. }) 8... d5 { He runs his d pawn two full squares forward, right past yours. Stop and look very carefully at what is legal here. } 9. exd6 { In passing! Your pawn takes as if his had only stepped one square, and the game stays level and rich for both of you. [%csl Gd6] } (9. Bb3 { Stepping the bishop back to safety on b three lets him keep his big pawn centre, and now it is a real, lasting asset. } 9... Bc5 10. Rd1 a5 11. a4 O-O { He settles into a comfortable position, while your own pawn on e five is left stuck and hard to support. }) (9. Rd1 { Look again before playing this: what exactly is happening to your own bishop, still sitting undefended on c four? } 9... dxc4 10. Qxc4 Ne7 11. Na3 Qd5 { You end up giving away a whole bishop in exchange for a single pawn — a poor trade by any measure. }) (9. Bb5 { Running the bishop sideways to b five is safe enough, but the moment to capture in passing has already gone. } 9... dxc3 10. Nxc3 a6 11. Ba4 b5 { He spends the next few moves kicking your pieces around while comfortably holding his own pawn centre. }) 1-0


[Event "Scotch Gambit: he holds the pawn with the bishop"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/89PLb4a2/BlDrqTWO"]
[FEN "r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/8/2BpP3/5N2/PPP2PPP/RNBQK2R b KQkq - 1 4"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/8/2BpP3/5N2/PPP2PPP/RNBQK2R_b_KQkq_-_1_4"]

{ Sometimes he simply settles for keeping the pawn he already has, rather than grabbing for even more. Watch closely what piece he holds it with. }
4... Bc5 { His bishop swings out to c five, both developing and quietly holding onto the extra pawn on d four. What is your plan against it? } 5. c3 { Nice — the little pawn attacks the extra pawn he is holding on d four. Win it back next move and two of your pawns stand side by side in the middle. [%cal Gc3d4] } (5. Ng5 { The knight lunges straight at f seven again, hunting for a quick trick. It is playable, but the resulting mess is hard to handle. } 5... Nh6 6. Bxf7+ Nxf7 7. Nxf7 Kxf7 { A queen check on h five soon wins the bishop back, so material ends up level — but the position stays wild and messy. }) (5. Bxf7+ { Look again before playing this sacrifice: what exactly does his king get to do once it recaptures the bishop? } 5... Kxf7 6. c3 d5 7. cxd4 Bb4+ { You have given away a whole bishop for this sacrifice, and his king ends up perfectly safe regardless. }) (5. O-O { Castling straight away is a sensible move in itself, but it does nothing to challenge the extra pawn he is holding. } 5... d6 6. c3 Bg4 7. Qb3 Bxf3 { This stays playable chess, but the resulting position is a great deal less clear than the pawn-offering line. }) 5... Nf6 { He develops his knight to f six, putting your e pawn under a little pressure. What about that pawn he is still holding on d four? } 6. cxd4 { Exactly — recapture the pawn, and now your two central pawns stand proudly side by side, controlling a wide swathe of the board. [%csl Gd4,Ge4] } (6. e5 { Pushing the other pawn forward is also good and well worth knowing — it kicks his knight straight out of the centre. } 6... d5 7. Bb5 Ne4 8. cxd4 Bb6 { This is just as good a plan in its own right, but today it is the two-pawn centre we are aiming for. }) (6. O-O { Castling before recapturing the pawn gives him a free move to grab your pawn on e four instead. } 6... Nxe4 7. cxd4 d5 8. dxc5 dxc4 { The position turns messy, and by the end of it he is still the one holding the extra pawn. }) (6. b4 { Hitting the bishop with a wing pawn looks aggressive, but it forgets all about the pawn still sitting in the middle. } 6... Bb6 7. a4 a6 8. O-O Nxe4 { He simply takes your pawn on e four in reply and ends up with the more comfortable position. }) 6... Bb4+ { His bishop swings out to b four with check. Several of your pieces could block it — which one is actually the best choice? } 7. Bd2 { The right piece for the job — the bishop blocks the check, offers a trade, and keeps your whole position solid and simple. } (7. Nbd2 { Blocking with the queen knight instead is also completely fine, and it develops a piece at the same time. } 7... Nxe4 8. O-O Nxd2 9. Bxd2 Bxd2 { Material stays equal here, and a whole flurry of pieces trade off the board along the way. }) (7. Nc3 { This is the old-fashioned gambit approach — you hand over the e pawn as well, purely for extra speed in development. } 7... Nxe4 8. O-O Bxc3 9. d5 Bf6 { This leads to a wild, sharp position that is genuinely playable — but today we are keeping things simple. }) (7. Kf1 { This blocks the check with nothing at all — your king simply steps aside, and the rook behind it stays fast asleep. } 7... d5 8. exd5 Nxd5 9. Nc3 Bxc3 { You end up worse off here for no good reason, having thrown away your own right to castle. }) 7... Bxd2+ { He trades the bishops off on d two with check. Two different pieces of yours can recapture — choose carefully between them. } 8. Nbxd2 { Perfect — the queen knight takes back, so your queen stays home and your last sleeping piece joins the game. Two pawns in the middle, every knight and bishop out. [%csl Gd2] } (8. Qxd2 { Recapturing with the queen looks natural, but she ends up sitting loose right in front of your own king. } 8... Nxe4 9. Qe2 Qe7 10. O-O Nf6 { He wins his pawn back with very little effort, and your queen's awkward post only makes things easier for him. }) (8. Nfxd2 { This is the wrong knight to recapture with — it leaves the other one stuck, and now look again at what happens to your pawn on d4. } 8... Nxd4 9. O-O d6 10. Nb3 Nxb3 11. axb3 { You win the knight back, but your proud pawn on d four has gone and he is a clean pawn ahead. }) (8. Kxd2 { Never recapture with the king here — look at exactly what arrives with check on the very next move if you do. } 8... Nxe4+ 9. Ke1 d5 10. Be2 O-O { You are a clean pawn down, and having moved your king by hand, your two rooks will never get the chance to connect for the rest of the game. }) 1-0


