[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/8/2BpP3/5N2/PPP2PPP/RNBQK2R_b_KQkq_-_1_4"]

{ Sometimes, instead of a quiet developing move, his bishop comes straight out with a check of its own. }
4... Bb4+ { He checks you straight away from b four, before doing anything else. How should you deal with this early check? } 5. c3 { Good — block the check with your pawn, and in the same move offer him a trade right in the middle of the board. [%cal Gc3d4] } (5. Bd2 { Blocking the check with your own bishop instead is perfectly fine too, and it leads to a calm, simple position. } 5... Bxd2+ 6. Qxd2 Nf6 7. Bd5 O-O { Material stays even here, and while this is a fine game, the pawn block fights for a little more. }) (5. Nbd2 { Blocking with the queen knight is playable, but it clogs up your own pieces and slows your development. } 5... Nf6 6. O-O O-O 7. e5 Ng4 { He settles into a perfectly comfortable position, with nothing of yours actively troubling him. }) (5. Kf1 { Moving your king to block the check throws away your right to castle for the rest of the game. } 5... Nf6 6. a3 Bc5 7. b4 Be7 { You end up worse off here for no good reason, having given up castling for nothing. }) 5... dxc3 { He grabs the pawn on c three. Two different pieces of yours could recapture it — which one is actually the right choice? } 6. bxc3 { Recapture with the b pawn — it covers the d four square, and your rook now has the b column in front of it, with only his b pawn in the way. [%cal Ga1b1] } (6. Nxc3 { Recapturing with the knight is also perfectly good — it develops a new piece at the very moment it captures. } 6... d6 7. Ng5 Nh6 8. O-O O-O { This stays even and perfectly pleasant, though recapturing with the pawn does keep a few more pawns on the board. }) (6. O-O { Castling straight away leaves the pawn alone entirely, and simply lets him keep it in his pocket for now. } 6... Nf6 7. bxc3 Bc5 8. e5 d5 { This stays perfectly playable chess, but the plain, immediate recapture is a much cleaner way to handle it. }) (6. Kf1 { Never move your king here instead of recapturing — he simply grabs yet another pawn on his very next move. } 6... cxb2 7. Bxb2 Nf6 8. e5 Nh5 { You end up two whole pawns down, and your own rook in the corner has nowhere useful left to go. }) 6... Ba5 { His bishop slides back to a five to stay on the board. Your own king, meanwhile, is still standing in the middle. } 7. O-O { Yes — your king is safe at last. The bishop still stares at f seven, and your rook can swing to b one and lean on his b pawn. } (7. Qb3 { Doubling up on f seven with the queen looks tempting and sharp, but he simply defends the pawn with ease. } 7... Qf6 8. O-O Nge7 9. Bg5 Qg6 { This stays playable chess, but bringing the queen out this early leaves her a target for his pieces. }) (7. Ba3 { Stopping him from castling with this bishop move looks clever, but the bishop itself ends up stranded and useless. } 7... Nge7 8. Ng5 d5 9. exd5 Ne5 { He calmly unwinds his position over the next few moves, and you are left with the worse side of the game. }) (7. Nbd2 { The knight settles onto a passive square here, doing very little for the rest of the game. } 7... Nge7 8. O-O h6 9. Bb3 O-O { This works out fine for him, and your own knight is left with no real future anywhere on the board. }) 1-0


[Event "Scotch Gambit: the quiet d6"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/89PLb4a2/WfUCmwvV"]
[FEN "r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/8/2BpP3/5N2/PPP2PPP/RNBQK2R b KQkq - 1 4"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/8/2BpP3/5N2/PPP2PPP/RNBQK2R_b_KQkq_-_1_4"]

{ Not every reply from him is sharp. Sometimes he simply tries to hang on to the extra pawn quietly, without stirring up any trouble. }
4... d6 { A quiet little pawn move to d six, played purely to defend the extra pawn on d four for now. How should you answer it? } 5. Nxd4 { Simple and strong — since he never grabbed for more, there is no need for a gambit here. Just take the pawn straight back. [%csl Gd4] } (5. c3 { You can offer a second pawn instead. He takes it, you develop fast — but nothing makes him give anything back. } 5... dxc3 6. Nxc3 Nf6 7. Qb3 Qd7 { Lively and playable, though he keeps the extra pawn and you are the one who has to prove something. }) (5. O-O { Castling first here is also good, and every bit as playable as recapturing the pawn immediately. } 5... Bg4 6. h3 Bxf3 7. Qxf3 Qd7 8. Qb3 { This reaches a genuinely pleasant position, but simply taking the pawn back is the far more straightforward plan. }) (5. Bb5 { Pinning the knight this way is playable, but it usually ends in trading off your best attacking bishop for it. } 5... Bd7 6. O-O Be7 7. Bxc6 Bxc6 { Material stays equal here, and along with the bishop trade, most of the fun in the position has gone too. }) (5. Ng5 { The familiar lunge toward f seven simply does not work in this position — he guards the square with ease. } 5... Nh6 6. Nf3 Qf6 7. Bb5 Qg6 { Your knight has spent two whole moves going back and forth, and there is nothing to show for the trip. }) 5... Nf6 { His knight comes out to f six, putting a little pressure on your e pawn. Your own king, though, is still sitting in the middle. } 6. O-O { Well done — king safe first. And the pawn on e four is not really hanging: if his knight grabs it, your rook swings to e one and pins it against his king. [%cal Gf1e1] } (6. Nc3 { Developing the last knight here is also good, and it guards the e pawn as a useful side effect. } 6... Be7 7. O-O O-O 8. a4 Re8 { This reaches a position that is just as pleasant, and honestly either move here is perfectly fine to play. }) (6. Nxc6 { Trading knights here mostly helps him — his b pawn gets to recapture toward the middle of the board. } 6... bxc6 7. Nc3 Be7 8. b3 a5 { This position is still fine for you, but the trade has given away all the tension that made it interesting. }) (6. Bg5 { Pinning the knight here achieves very little — he simply breaks the pin himself on the very next move. } 6... h6 7. Bxf6 Qxf6 8. Nb5 Qd8 { You have ended up trading a genuinely good bishop for one of his knights, which is no bargain at all. }) 6... Be7 { He tucks his bishop quietly onto e seven, ready to castle next move. Notice which one of your own pieces is still missing. } 7. Nc3 { Perfect — the last knight comes out and guards your pawn on e four. Both knights and the bishop are working, your king is safe, and the middlegame can start. [%csl Gc3] } (7. Nxc6 { Trading knights again here just repeats the earlier idea — his b pawn marches to the middle and helps his position. } 7... bxc6 8. Nc3 a5 9. b3 O-O { This stays perfectly fine for you, but once again you have helped his pawn structure along for no reason. }) (7. Re1 { Bringing the rook to the centre is a useful move in itself, but developing the last knight really ought to come first. } 7... Nxd4 8. Qxd4 O-O 9. Nc3 Ng4 { This position is still good for you, just a touch slower to reach than developing the knight straight away. }) (7. Bb5 { Pinning the knight this way gives away your best attacking bishop yet again, just as it did earlier in this line. } 7... Bd7 8. Nxc6 Bxc6 9. Bxc6+ bxc6 { Material stays equal here, and once more the attacking bishop that made your position dangerous has disappeared. }) 1-0


[Event "Scotch Gambit: he takes with the knight"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
