[FEN "r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/4p3/3PP3/5N2/PPP2PPP/RNBQKB1R b KQkq d3 0 3"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/4p3/3PP3/5N2/PPP2PPP/RNBQKB1R_b_KQkq_d3_0_3"]

{ Sometimes, instead of the natural pawn recapture, he chooses to recapture with a piece instead. }
3... Nxd4 { He recaptures the pawn with his knight instead of his own e pawn. Do not just grab it straight back — stop and look first. } 4. Nxd4 { Right — trade the knights off first. Only once his knight is gone does the square d four become genuinely safe to hold. [%csl Gd4] } (4. Qxd4 { Look again before playing this: what exactly is his own e pawn attacking right now, if you bring the queen there? } 4... exd4 { His pawn simply eats your queen for free — always trade the knights off before you even think about the queen. }) (4. Nxe5 { You can grab the pawn on e five too, and it is playable, but your knight then gets chased all around the board. } 4... Ne6 5. Nc4 b5 6. Ne3 Bb7 { This reaches a sound position in theory, but it is far harder to handle over the board than our simple line. }) (4. Bc4 { Bringing the gambit bishop out here looks thematic, but the right moment for it has already passed by. } 4... Nxf3+ 5. Qxf3 Qe7 6. Nc3 c6 { He has simply traded a piece off cleanly and is left with a perfectly comfortable position. }) 4... exd4 { He recaptures with his own pawn this time. Now the pawn on d four is standing there completely alone, with nothing guarding it. } 5. Qxd4 { Yes — now the queen may safely take the pawn, and from d four she stands proudly in the middle of the whole board. [%csl Gd4] } (5. Bc4 { Leaving the pawn alone for now and developing the bishop instead is also perfectly playable. } 5... d5 6. Bxd5 Nf6 7. Qxd4 Be7 { This reaches a good game too, but the pawn on d four really was sitting there completely free for the taking. }) (5. Be2 { A quiet developing move that lets him hold onto the extra pawn for a while longer, since nothing challenges it yet. } 5... Bc5 6. O-O a5 7. c3 dxc3 { He grabs a second pawn on the way past — two pawns up now, and perfectly comfortable. }) (5. c3 { Offering yet another pawn here is simply not needed — the queen can recapture the first one safely on its own. } 5... d5 6. Qxd4 dxe4 7. Qxe4+ Be7 { Material stays level, but you have given away your whole central presence for nothing in return. }) 5... Qf6 { His queen comes out to offer a trade and puts your own queen under attack at the same time. Where should she go? } 6. Qc4 { Good — she sidesteps the trade and lands on the diagonal that points straight at f seven, the weak square this whole opening is built to attack. [%csl Gf7] } (6. Qxf6 { Trading queens is safe and perfectly level, but it develops his knight for him and takes every bit of sting out of the gambit. } 6... Nxf6 7. Nc3 Bb4 8. Bd2 d6 { Dead level, no attack left, and a long quiet afternoon ahead of you. }) (6. e5 { Kicking his queen with the pawn is just as good a move. The queens usually come off, and his rook gets a wide open column in return. } 6... Qb6 7. Qxb6 axb6 8. Nc3 c6 { Material stays equal, and his rook has the open a column to use for the rest of the game. }) (6. Qe3 { Retreating the queen to e three is also fine, though from there she blocks your own dark-squared bishop's best diagonal. } 6... Bb4+ 7. c3 Be7 8. Bd3 Qh6 { This stays pleasant enough for you, just a little clumsy compared with the cleaner square on c four. }) (6. Qc3 { Offering a straight queen trade here is perfectly safe, but it drains almost all the ambition out of the position. } 6... Qxc3+ 7. Nxc3 Bb4 8. Bd2 d6 { Material stays even, and with the queens both gone, most of the fun has drained right out of it. }) 1-0
